6045417] [17592186162912 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162912 #edb/ref 1061 31345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162912 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0a6dc33-d7cd-5300-a862-451162ab2de3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162912 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162913 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162913 #edb/ref 1061 31348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162913 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c7ff20bf-405e-5d22-a1a1-24febcd398fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162913 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162914 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162914 #edb/ref 1061 31349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162914 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1d953a4-69ca-563a-afef-090b7f058ef3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162914 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163053 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163053 #edb/ref 1061 37257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163053 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c32bd79-d939-5ba0-afca-75b88e74b8b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163053 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163155 #edb/ref 1061 42397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163155 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7cc03796-6cdf-582b-9c6a-edc187a827b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163155 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163232 #edb/ref 1064 "The **singular value decomposition** (or SVD) of an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ is a factorization of the form\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m \\times m$ matrix, and\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n \\times n$ matrix.\n\nThe columns of $U$ are called the **left singular vectors** of $A,$ and the columns of $V$ are called the **right singular vectors** of $A.$\n\nFor example, the matrix ![](images/46/46b29ceb84e2ed98e48642e67277c9bf63d0552bcd07f813a9ffcaf665ff91b7.svg) has the following singular value decomposition:\n\n![](images/29/298933eb0a789ebe57a296088172f6927d1573770824c0b0cffb393eb9894c70.svg)\n\nThe singular value decomposition of a matrix $A$ can be thought of as a type of diagonalization that works for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix! It's also one of linear algebra's most useful forms of matrix factorization with applications in statistics, image compression, machine learning, and more." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163232 #edb/ref 1065 4892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163232 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ae8b1a4c-c37e-5527-9f72-9cf20f5af926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163232 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163246 #edb/ref 1064 "We can now fully describe the algorithm for computing the singular value decomposition of $A.$\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A.$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. This allows us to construct the second matrix $\\Sigma.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$). This allows us to construct the third matrix $V.$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ using the singular values, right singular vectors, and the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. This allows us to construct the matrix $U.$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown below. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ let's find the matrices $U$ and $V.$\n\n![](images/ab/abe87fb2a304ff102bb3f703d025c7d41f56cb6fab7fc477b3073cbd820cda6e.svg)\n\nWe find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using our algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & =[\\begin{aligned}89 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 113\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=11$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=9.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 121I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n![](images/41/4195807c3f61cd730c57340666c8d651e94d2b277f9623da7cd482c1d7123303.svg)\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n![](images/64/64aa31f039ec188355522cffa8a6c62df8c53bb409e55df32ce880d9a9efcd6e.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ab/ab0a5a5a284c962414158e5b7c463264b271bdd57decd8e08305285f7243f7bd.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163246 #edb/ref 1065 5010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163246 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4b48ac70-7c4e-59be-bdfb-64125ae50eec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163246 #edb/ref 1067 "An Algorithm for Finding a Singular Value Decomposition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163247 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, and \n\n$$\nA= U\\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's describe how to find the matrices $V$ and $\\Sigma$ of this singular value decomposition. \n\nNotice that if we compute $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={(UΣ{V}^{T})}^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =(V{Σ}^{T}\\,{U}^{T})(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}\\,U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}({I}_{n})Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V({Σ}^{T}\\,Σ){V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we note that $A^T \\! A$ is always symmetric, and that $\\Sigma^T \\! \\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix with the squares of the singular values on the main diagonal. As a result,\n\n$$\nA^TA = V\\left( \\Sigma^T \\Sigma\\right) V^T\n$$\n\nis an orthogonal diagonalization of the symmetric matrix $A^T \\! A.$ So, to find the matrix $V$ in our SVD, we need to orthogonally diagonalize $A^T A.$\n\nLet's demonstrate how this works in practice by computing the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ in the SVD of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/b7/b766ec7d8c00dfc121e707566d21db470c087c3e785f4e1b2868e7f932873c67.svg)\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- Computing $A^T \\! A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving the characteristic equation $|A^T \\! A - \\lambda I| = 0,$ we get the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\lambda_1 = 16}}, \\qquad  {\\color{red}{\\lambda_2 = 1}}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding singular values are \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}  = 4},  \\qquad {\\color{red}\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} = 1}.\n$$\n\nWe now find a unit eigenvector basis of $A^T \\! A.$ These unit eigenvectors will be the columns of our matrix $V$ in the orthogonal diagonalization of $A^T \\! A$.\n\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{blue}\\lambda_1} I),$ we obtain\n![](images/f0/f074943374fd488663eda967ab64f335047f9b6cc91c98c504dcb0f2ee8282a6.svg)\nSeeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - 16I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get\n![](images/03/03d6a116d751736f50c366cd4721ee7d1dbf39048092272acc6577934f4600c7.svg)\n\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{red}\\lambda_2} I),$ we obtain\n![](images/16/16813fa19b3417b6de49f9c3c1c27cfedca850b473da01d3b0e0a0b3091b6e28.svg)\nSeeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get\n![](images/32/32b3c9740740767d4d849ac2d73552dee1e225cc507e95a17c727e8c3fcc6275.svg)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/85/85c926521f0588117992c019ab0db3723520a14ba4c2879d630ab391462b4af2.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163247 #edb/ref 1065 5011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163247 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bbaa68b2-45d9-56a5-99b7-90dce5ec08da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163247 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Second and Third Matrices of a Singular Value Decomposition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163248 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is an ![](images/25/2521651122403a440e682fef383af333958f5b0d1334abe8b0771865d4daf100.svg) matrix, and \n\n![](images/ec/ec033e8518a97ff363494df7effa3c6f032b7cfaa028712b87959d659ea12962.svg)\nis a singular value decomposition of ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg) Let's now describe how to compute the matrix ![](images/c3/c3ae10e66e0dee45c359329a1cd8b0246634f95fdce2f5bf206d04dac3594fc5.svg) of the SVD using the matrices ![](images/12/128e4b474ccbb86ef9b1a105a348e493595f4084d05e9cfa5f7889d390d5eac4.svg) and ![](images/4d/4d936a1af541b15fe85bff8b60fbe1d8d1851f9e91616cf4799dd0c5323d038a.svg)\n\nRecall that ![](images/be/bef27a67f1f86637c8224c0d929308a97b23aad62dfbbc5549c68e12bb94f143.svg) is orthogonal, meaning that ![](images/c7/c71eaab408a38ca1a1d0c653fe51ab02287d8ffbe4ef0461c93186579552d456.svg) Consequently, multiplying ![](images/c9/c99b0facd0ff831056deed9aaa5356ca5cbeed72ed9f632604cd5fb9479c3b7a.svg) by ![](images/be/bef27a67f1f86637c8224c0d929308a97b23aad62dfbbc5549c68e12bb94f143.svg) on the right, we obtain\n\n![](images/03/036ae766d4187370271793b6bbacc9841b444173ff031740acbd259531ed5126.svg)\n\nIn expanded form, our final equation looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b9/b924929217b058d3edefff9a6557d3ad8b5d6b3536e7ac0dcaf185e50b60bb5a.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/64/64b1897c8532aee8fee0d4f76f632cc91c6a5a4078b11bfaaea07235aeadcc3f.svg)\n\nfor ![](images/fe/fedbe16eb8c09802bf435c256bb225249c1ee578dd558d0f3b6b284a56236517.svg) Finally, provided that ![](images/66/668ce05b1a81d3d120c47a7dadea446891ef986edb5f499c592f310755bb143c.svg) we obtain \n\n![](images/ec/ecd9fe71eaef2172512d70bfb78ffea6e36b973e6f96c09f0480ef2e0e9e55ad.svg) \n\nWe can use this to find the columns of the matrix ![](images/f9/f9d412fae57d02f4fc44b04a277e1a6512e3f560e3152416a69d30d72e38a9ac.svg) Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163248 #edb/ref 1065 5012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163248 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b577630f-1060-52c1-aabe-91b641046fa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163248 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the First Matrix of a Singular Value Decomposition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163317 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown below. \n\n![](images/61/61ff6d3a83c2a660ca8369d96ab7bede86f9a124727db8d5a7e320dcd5aaad74.svg)\n\nSuppose we're given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's use this information to find the matrix $U.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=1.$ \n\nEarlier, we saw that the columns of $\\mathbf u$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. Therefore, we compute the columns of $\\mathbf u$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute the vector $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the vector $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the matrix $U$ in our singular value decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n![](images/15/159ace613dacf666b32131543c77b3eb602e16226ba2d37e9e8bea4dcff1cb56.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163317 #edb/ref 1065 6135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163317 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "04644a93-8cce-5c2e-b575-d7f05b4985df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163317 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the First Matrix - A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163364 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $A = U \\Sigma V^T$ be a singular value decomposition of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ with singular values $\\sigma_1$ and $\\sigma_2.$ \n\nDue to the decomposition, the action of $A$ on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is the same as the action of the sequence $V^T \\to \\Sigma \\to U.$\n\nAs shown below, let's consider both equivalent actions on a unit circle (with the two standard basis vectors). \n\n![](images/18/18bf28d59138e6c50e950493570a4e9caa8ec2f86eb81d573272cb4677ea1d69.png)\n\n- Since $V^T$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix, it represents a transformation of the plane that preserves distances between vectors. This could be a rotation, a reflection, or a combination of these two, which is sometimes called a **rotoreflection.**\n\n- Since $\\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix, it represents a scaling along the $x$- and $y$-axes by $\\sigma_1$ and $\\sigma_2,$ respectively.\n\n- Since $U$ is again an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix, it represents a rotation, a reflection, or a rotoreflection.\n\nTo summarize, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Any linear transformation on the plane can be decomposed into an orthogonal transformation (rotation, reflection, or rotoreflection), a scaling, and another orthogonal transformation (rotation, reflection, or rotoreflection).*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163364 #edb/ref 1065 7033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163364 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4cadc01a-8396-532a-a438-479affeb9a04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163364 #edb/ref 1067 "Geometric Interpretation of the Singular Value Decomposition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371283 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371283 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371283 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371283 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}68 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 29\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371283 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f6a8969-0c0e-547e-babb-6ff2c612e774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371283 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=16.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-16I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-16I=[\\begin{aligned}68 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 29\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}52 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371284 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371284 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371284 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371284 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "06d08e03-206a-52a5-b561-b040a6082be8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371285 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371285 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371285 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e784017-9f26-57fb-8d09-e2c85b00c13e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371286 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371286 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371286 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c0fd7571-0b27-57c2-bd8b-9ae270401cc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371287 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371287 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371287 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "053e86da-1624-5e0f-a032-984baf156135" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371288 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371288 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371288 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7a7c0920-0814-53cc-a6b5-0e0aded26986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371289 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371289 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371289 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "52d8a537-0c37-5e79-800b-5a598b1c9e12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371360 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371360 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96303" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371360 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371360 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371360 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e6bed9f-9e8e-54a8-ba26-f0e8954ce7e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371360 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371361 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371361 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371361 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371361 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371361 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371361 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371361 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371361 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371361 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "64e997c7-7524-5cf1-946e-c03af754bbbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371362 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371362 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371362 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b340a6b9-2150-5d09-b212-4169a2e45650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371363 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371363 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371363 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ed71c6ee-fc7e-5366-b928-3a0e8ac31c83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371364 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371364 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371364 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3decd7ef-d6cb-5d2c-a821-182982c2d6d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371365 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371365 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371365 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f1659e51-bced-5253-9862-0e84267f1efe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371366 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371366 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371366 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a7eb61e-6225-5628-bd68-d73407f01588" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404836 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404836 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404836 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404836 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404836 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "069d96cb-6a82-58f7-9653-ba67b55165b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404836 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404837 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404837 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404837 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404837 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404837 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404837 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404837 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404837 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186404839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404837 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ca27652a-1905-5111-863a-956b813d6f8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404838 #edb/ref 1080 "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404838 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404838 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e43e58b-e171-5bde-8e66-fa1047e3a5e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404839 #edb/ref 1080 "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404839 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404839 #